WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.319
My name is Stephen Naron, and I'm

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.369
the director of the Fortunoff Video

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.109
Archive for Holocaust Testimonies,

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:13.819
and it's my pleasure to open the

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.069
second annual Geoffrey Hartman

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.760
Fellowship Symposium.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.809
I hope this day will prove to be

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.239
an enriching and productive one.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.249
I'd like to thank the Fortunoff

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.285
family, and in particular Joshua and

00:00:27.286 --> 00:00:28.429
Esther, for being here today,

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:31.069
and all of the other donors,

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:32.508
Blaustein, Libby and Ross families,

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:33.829
and dozens of others, large and

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:35.719
small, who helped make the Archive's

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.720
work possible.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.589
The Fortunoff Archive's Hartman

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:41.059
Fellowship program, now in its

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.919
second year, was established

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.809
in honor of Geoffrey Hartman,

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.979
passed away on March 14th, 2016.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.639
An eminent professor of comparative

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:50.989
literature at Yale and the Fortunoff

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:52.819
Video Archive's long time faculty

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.709
advisor, Geoffrey's vision

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:55.849
and leadership helped build the

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.139
Fortunoff Archive into the

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.630
institution that it is today.

00:01:00.319 --> 00:01:01.519
The Fellowship, now in its second

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.529
year is a 12 month fellowship,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.119
postdoctoral fellowship, for a

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.669
single new PhD.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.709
In this year, we expanded the

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.479
program to include a joint

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.859
fellowship with the Vienna

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.479
Wiesenthal Institute.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.189
And I'd like to think Eva Kovacs and

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:17.809
her husband Janusz for coming all

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.879
the way from Vienna to,

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.159
to, to be here today to

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.409
celebrate this initial

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.479
VWI/Fortunoff joint

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:27.480
fellowship.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.199
Eva is the research program director

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.000
at the Vienna Wiesenthal Institute.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:34.859
The purpose of the Hartman

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.899
Fellowship is to provide scholars

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.729
with an opportunity to pursue

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.319
deep research in the Fortunoff

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.179
collection to advance their own

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.399
scholarship, as well as to produce

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.259
an annotated critical edition of a

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:47.260
testimony.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.009
Now, this critical edition is

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.719
a testimony transcript with

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.429
annotations in an introductory

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.430
chapter.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.039
This, these annotations

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.169
in this chapter are, are designed

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:01.639
to illuminate the complexities of

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.439
the narrative and to include

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.359
additional information and context

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.399
to help readers better

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.379
understand or grasp the complexity

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.063
of a document like a testimony in

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:12.680
our, from our collection.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:16.519
The essay, the introductory essay

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.139
is designed to focus on the

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.609
significance of this testimony for

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.559
Holocaust history in general,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.539
as well as the fellow's scholarship

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.549
in particular, and our hope

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.049
is that our fellows will also, after

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.969
leaving here, serve as ambassadors

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.949
to, for the collection as

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:31.939
they continue to use it in their

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.799
research and teaching

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.809
at the next institution that

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:36.810
they call home.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.189
The critical editions,

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.199
I hope, unlock testimony

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.429
and make them accessible to a

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.409
broader readership and

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:46.939
encourage both scholarly and

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.739
educational use of these resources

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:49.969
at various levels.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.650
We have two presentations today

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:55.959
and then will, each of them will be

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.249
followed by a Q&amp;A and we'll take a

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:58.899
break between them as well so people

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.149
can get refreshments.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.069
And the first speaker

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:04.870
will be Sari Siegel, who is our

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:07.129
second Geoffrey H.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:08.289
Hartman fellow.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:09.459
Sari has an M.A.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:10.599
and a Ph.D. in history from the

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.240
University of Southern California.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.299
And she received her B.A.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.300
from Yale University.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:18.729
Boola boola.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:19.722
The Geoffrey H.

00:03:19.723 --> 00:03:20.919
Hartman postdoctoral fellowship

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:21.920
brings her back.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.869
She earned her B.A. in 2006.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.519
In addition to receiving numerous

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:28.989
grants and fellowships, she has been

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.609
a fellow in residence at the Center

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:31.689
for Holocaust Studies at the

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.029
Institute for Contemporary History.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.199
She's been a fellow at the Vienna

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.789
Wiesenthal Institute in the U.S.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.289
Holocaust Memorial Museum Jack,

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:39.849
Joseph, and Morton Mandel Center for

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:41.090
Advanced Holocaust Studies.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.019
She has presented her research on

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.699
Jewish prisoner physicians at many

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.259
conferences and workshops in the

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.309
U.S. and abroad.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.489
Sari will be presenting her work on

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.349
the testimony of Edith F..

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:54.729
So please join me.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:55.784
Oh, Esther. Sorry, Esther F.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.819
So please join me in welcoming Sari.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.159
Thank you for the introduction.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.199
Stephen, I would also like to take

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.239
this opportunity to thank

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.459
you for the fellowship,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.119
uh, uh opportunity

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.299
and to thank the donors who

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.398
have created this position

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:19.439
in the memory of the very

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.109
important scholar Geoffrey Hartman.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.119
It has been a pleasure to be a part

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.069
of the Fortunoff Video Archive,

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.019
especially as it marks

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.849
40 years since the taping of the

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.069
first four testimonies

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.170
with this special series of events.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.150
Thanks also go to Christy Tomecek

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.099
for her assistance with the clips

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.079
and to Debra Bush for her assistance

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.579
with just about everything else.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.510
Esther was born Estera Nysenhauz

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:56.329
on August 16, 1908,

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:58.549
in Lodz, or Lodz, Poland.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.339
On October 9, 1991,

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.469
when she was eighty three years old,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.629
Esther sat for an interview

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.669
with Jaschael Pery at

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.559
the Museum of Jewish Heritage in New

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.560
York.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.449
Esther grew up in a traditional

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.279
Jewish home which kept

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.469
kosher and observed Shabbat

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:20.470
but was not Hasidic.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.639
She was fluent in Yiddish and

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.499
Polish, and attended

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.749
a private Jewish school with Polish

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.360
as the language of instruction.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.239
Unlike the majority of her peers,

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.309
she was not affiliated

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:36.839
with youth groups or political

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:37.840
parties.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.629
As a result of living, living

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:42.399
in a predominantly Jewish

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.259
neighborhood and attending a Jewish

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.199
school, she had limited encounters

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.179
with non-Jewish Poles and

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.829
did not personally experience

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:51.830
antisemitism.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.129
As a result of the numerus clausus

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.959
that severely limited Jews'

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.269
access to medical education

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.339
within Poland, Esther traveled

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:03.539
to France.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.669
She returned to Lodz with

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.679
her medical degree in

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:08.680
1933.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.829
Interested in pediatrics, she

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:12.809
worked at a children's hospital

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:14.879
but then switched to a position

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.679
as a doctor for a workers union.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.919
Shortly before the German invasion

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.869
of Poland, Esther married

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.729
a school principal whom she

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:25.829
last saw when he left for duty

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.379
as an officer in the Polish army.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.959
He was captured shortly thereafter

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.879
and as a Jewish POW,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.799
eventually set to the, sent

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.289
to the Wlodawa ghetto,

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.119
from which he was later deported

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.120
to Sobibor.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:46.279
Esther and other residents of

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.912
Lodz, renamed Litzmannstadt

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.079
by German officials, were

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.609
forced to move into the city's

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:54.559
ghetto when it was first

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.709
established in February 1940.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:59.089
She worked in one of the ghetto's

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:01.369
hospitals and as a member

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.169
of the ghetto's emergency medical

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:04.999
service, making house

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.159
calls in response to reported

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.139
medical emergencies.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:10.819
She lived in the ghetto with her

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:13.249
mother and one of her brothers,

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.379
all of whom were deported together

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.209
to Auschwitz when the ghetto

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.759
was liquidated in August,

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:20.760
1944.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:23.479
Over those four and a half years,

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.189
she and nearly all ghetto

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:27.529
inhabitants suffered from hunger,

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.829
cold, and fear of deportations.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.799
This image shows the doctors,

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.259
nurses and emergency personnel

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:36.509
of the hospital on...

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.549
going to try to do the Polish,

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:41.410
Lagiewnicka Street.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:43.779
It is unclear whether she is

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:44.780
pictured here.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.009
In August 1944, during

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:51.539
the final liquidation of the

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.489
Litzmannstadt Ghetto, Esther

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.379
was deported, along with her brother

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:58.229
and mother, to Auschwitz-Birkenau,

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.269
where she spent five days in

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:02.609
a section designated for Jews

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.589
to be transferred to other camps and

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:06.149
utilized for slave labor.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:09.189
When a call went out for doctors,

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:11.199
she identified herself

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:13.749
and was subsequently sent to work

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:15.609
as a doctor in Guben,

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.109
a subcamp connected to the

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.029
Gross-Rosen concentration camp.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.569
Located on the current border

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:24.669
between Germany and Poland,

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.499
this camp housed young women

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:29.619
assigned to work on radio equipment

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.839
for the German Air Force.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.788
After the camp was evacuated

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:35.408
due to the approach of the Soviet

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:38.139
army in early February

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.178
1945, she traveled several

00:08:40.179 --> 00:08:42.308
days in an uncovered truck

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:44.349
and arrived at Bergen-Belsen

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:45.730
on February 15th.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.159
She received an assignment

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.169
from the head prisoner physician,

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:53.269
but came down with typhus within two

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:55.669
weeks and was still recovering

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:57.349
when the British army liberated the

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.599
camp on April 15th,

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:00.889
1945.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:05.439
She moved the short distance

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:06.819
to the Bergen-Belsen Displaced

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.009
Persons Camp, which was established

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:11.319
in nearby Army barracks.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.179
Once she recovered, she

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.099
provided medical attention to her

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:16.929
fellow survivors.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.819
Not desiring to return to her

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:20.859
native Poland, she decided

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.779
to volunteer to be among a group

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.729
of DPs to go to Sweden for

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.859
further rehabilitation.

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:29.979
They departed in July 1945,

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:31.779
and she continued her medical work

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.679
in Sweden, treating Jewish DPs.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:37.359
Her work came to a halt, however,

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.729
once her fellow DP's had recovered

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:40.730
and dispersed.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.509
She subsequently benefited from

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:45.369
the kindness of a prominent Jewish

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:47.289
doctor in Stockholm and

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:50.109
worked in his forensic medicine lab.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:52.059
Over this period, she entered into

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:53.979
correspondence with

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:56.469
her husband's-- first husband's

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.389
best friend, who was living in

00:09:58.390 --> 00:09:59.379
New York.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.539
Shortly after he arrived in Sweden

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:03.639
to visit her, they were married.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.339
And in May 1947,

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:09.199
she followed him to New York,

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:10.879
where she eventually set up a

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:13.759
medical practice in pediatrics.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:15.649
She recorded her testimony for the

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:17.689
Fortunoff Video Archive on October

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:19.669
9, 1991, and

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:21.919
then for the Shoah Foundation Visual

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.199
History Archive on January

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:25.929
30, 1995.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:30.739
So, why did I choose Esther's

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:32.239
testimony?

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.249
Given that my dissertation studied

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:36.199
Jewish prisoner physicians in Nazi

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.509
camps and then my next project

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.339
will examine Holocaust survivors'

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:42.799
provision of medical care

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:44.569
to fellow survivors in the early

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.519
postwar years, Esther's

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.429
testimony seemed like an ideal fit.

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:51.499
After all, she served as a doctor

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.659
in Guben and utilized her medical

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:55.309
training to help survivors in

00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:57.229
Bergen-Belsen, and then

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:59.449
in DP camps in Sweden.

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:02.059
Upon further exploration though,

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:04.189
her testimony did not yield very

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:06.049
much insight into her medical work

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:07.789
in these venues.

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:09.799
Nevertheless, I decided to work

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:10.800
with HVT-2033,

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:14.119
because as a result of my

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.979
dissertation research, I was

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:18.169
attuned to a few threads

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:19.669
that appear throughout Esther's

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:21.139
testimony.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:23.629
Furthermore, the threads align

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:25.759
with my methodological approach,

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.799
the simultaneous investigation

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.079
of Jewish doctors on micro

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:31.489
and macro levels.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:36.969
Because Jewish doctors' medical

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:38.559
training provided them with

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:40.479
knowledge and the skill

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:42.489
set required to treat

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:44.319
the sick and injured in ghettos and

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.239
camps, their recruitment

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:48.609
and/or protection offers,

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:50.679
at least to some extent,

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:52.629
insight into the value accorded

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:54.519
to the lives of their prospective

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.049
patients.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:57.999
In turn, one can discern

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.369
dynamics between the pursuits

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:02.769
of two different yet overlapping

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:05.049
initiatives: the exploitation

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:07.479
of the Jewish labor force and

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.700
the extermination of European Jewry.

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:12.729
This line of reasoning is not

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:14.679
without exceptions, of course,

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:16.269
as Jewish doctors were also

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:18.309
mobilized for the benefit of

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:20.619
German officials' own health.

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:22.989
However, my dissertation research

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:24.909
revealed that this motivation

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.979
was generally secondary

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:29.079
to the goal of exploiting the Jewish

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:30.339
labor force.

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:32.589
I can address this caveat, as well

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:34.149
as the impact of insufficient

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:35.979
medical supplies, in the Q&amp;A.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:41.899
So how can Esther's testimony

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:43.939
offer insight into the macro

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:45.769
level dynamics between these

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:47.016
two major initiatives?

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:49.789
The first three sites of her

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:52.169
internment are crucial.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:53.789
The question of preserving or

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:56.189
liquidating Litzmannstadt Ghetto

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:57.959
was one of the key flashpoints

00:12:57.960 --> 00:12:59.999
between the German officials most

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.159
intent on extermination

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.079
and those more invested in

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:06.059
harnessing Jewish labor power for

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.069
financial gain and/or for

00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:09.929
the benefit of the German war

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:11.279
effort.

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.439
Esther's references to official

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:15.689
measures taken to preserve the lives

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:17.939
of ghetto doctors and to reinforce

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:19.229
the medical staff, more

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:21.059
specifically, the instructions to

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.099
perimeter guards not to shoot those

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.559
wearing particular medical insignia,

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:27.209
and the facilitation of the

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.129
recruitment of additional doctors

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.259
from the Warsaw Ghetto in

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.260
mid-1941,

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:35.219
communicate that doctors

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.739
had value to German officials

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:39.629
as possible preservers

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:40.830
of the labor force.

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:44.579
Furthermore, the ghettos

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:46.469
Vartegau location is

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.749
important as this Reich annexed

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.999
region of western Poland

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.919
was the manifestation, or saw

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:55.199
the manifestation of this conflict

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.269
in the simultaneous proliferation

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.279
of forced labor camps for Jews

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:01.499
in 1941, '42,

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.479
and '43, and

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:05.339
the operation of the Nazis first

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:07.619
extermination site Chelmno,

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:10.889
beginning in December 1941.

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:12.989
My dissertation research reveals

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:14.909
that each level of the civil

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:16.799
government in the Vartegau,

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.659
including municipal authorities

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:19.660
in charge of the Litzmannstadt
ghetto,

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:23.309
as well as officials in Berlin

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:25.289
and German businesses, the

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.299
businessmen, sorry, in

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.369
public and private industry

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.439
either instigated

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:33.719
or facilitated the transfer

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:36.089
of Jewish doctors from Berlin

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.189
to forced labor camps for Jews

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:40.169
in the name of preserving the labor

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:41.170
force.

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:43.799
When Esther arrived in Birkenau in

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:45.689
August 1944,

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:47.999
and during the preceding late spring

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.069
and summer months, the camp's

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.229
crematoria were working at and

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:54.149
beyond capacity

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:55.979
as transports arrived from

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:58.109
the Lodz and-- from the Lodz ghetto

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:00.089
and from Hungary.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:01.949
At the same time, however,

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:03.569
new sections of the camp were

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.519
established to hold thousands

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:07.619
of new arrivals who were soon

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.809
to be designated for transport

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:11.669
to hundreds of camps

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.769
to help the German war effort.

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:16.019
So while her mother was immediately

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.479
dispatched to the gas chamber,

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:21.119
Esther was among those sent

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.039
to the so-called transit camps

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:24.989
within Birkenau, and she was

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:26.909
transferred to Guben only five

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:27.910
days later.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:31.420
Her transfer from Birkenau

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:34.249
to serve as a doctor for Jews

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.589
working in a factory manufacturing

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:39.019
airplane radio parts, demonstrates

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:41.599
a clear interest in the preservation

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:43.549
of Jews' labor capacity.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.459
The camp's location within the Reich

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:48.979
proper draws further attention

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:50.389
to the importance of the Jewish

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:52.249
labor force as the Jews

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:54.529
were brought back into the territory

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:56.929
that was previously declared to be,

00:15:56.930 --> 00:15:59.059
and was meant to stay,

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:00.949
Judenrein, cleansed of

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:01.950
Jews.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:04.329
Guben was just one of

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:06.399
around one hundred Gross-Rosen

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.229
subcamps, dozens of

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.539
which contained prisoner populations

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:13.029
comprised mostly or entirely

00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:14.589
of Jews.

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:16.689
Once she arrived,

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:18.879
her experience communicates

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:20.844
that, at least to some extent,

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:24.049
the German official in charge of the

00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:25.909
camp had an interest in the

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:27.979
medical treatment of the inmates.

00:16:33.614 --> 00:16:36.099
Is the

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:37.589
volume, OK?

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:38.590
Yeah,

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:42.549
where, would it be from the computer

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:43.550
itself?

00:16:44.264 --> 00:16:45.264
You would do it from here.

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:47.970
Let's see.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:50.200
Didn't seem loud enough.

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:51.420
Let's try that again.

00:16:56.140 --> 00:16:57.140
I remember, Cibalgine--

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:58.058
There we are! Thank you.

00:16:58.059 --> 00:16:59.859
at that time was stylish, from Ciba,

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:01.359
Cibalgine is like an aspirin.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:03.329
So I called, like holding on

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:04.779
something, Cibagine.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.269
Alright I read the one word.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:08.108
The 'ine.--' er-- gave

00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:09.909
me the holding.

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:10.910
And I wrote 'aspirin.'

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:13.629
Oh, so there were the two things.

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:15.309
And sometimes a minute later, or

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.358
whatever, I wrote "cotton,"

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:18.450
"alcohol," "iodine,"

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:20.649
and that's all.

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:22.279
Maybe another word, I don't

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:24.249
remember. And she came

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:26.169
and she says to me,

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:28.149
"that's all what you ask right

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:29.150
now?"

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.099
Evidently, the female officer

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.959
in charge of the camp was prepared

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.969
to provide more supplies

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:37.799
as she asked Esther if the

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.719
doctor had listed all the supplies

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:40.720
she required.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.679
Her experience echoes my findings

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:45.149
related to Jewish prisoner

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.099
physicians within the industrial

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:48.989
subcamps of Auschwitz.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.029
Surviving doctors report that

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:53.189
on a variety of occasions,

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:55.799
SS medical orderlies or doctors

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.629
obtained requested medical

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:00.119
equipment or medications.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.559
And the importance of the Jewish

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.229
doctors to the industrial program

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.269
is clear from their transfers

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:08.639
from Birkenau to the subcamps

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:10.859
and from one subcamp to another.

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:16.969
I would also like to draw attention

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:19.189
to a micro thread that runs

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:21.409
throughout Esther's testimony,

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:23.809
the conduct of Jewish functionaries

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:25.129
and the issue of judgment.

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:28.459
Although admittedly problematic

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:30.319
and thus a possible topic for

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:32.359
discussion in the Q&amp;A, I'm

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:33.949
offering the following tentative

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.739
definition of Jewish functionaries:

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:39.739
Jews who held privileged positions

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:41.899
and carried out tasks that

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:44.089
in one way or another and to varying

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:45.919
extents, facilitated

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:47.479
the achievement of the German

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:48.480
officials' aims.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.569
As such, they

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.699
existed in what Holocaust survivor

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:56.789
and author Primo Levi calls

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.009
"the gray zone," a, quote,

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:00.929
"poorly defined space where the

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:02.849
two camps of masters and

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:04.769
servants both diverge and

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:07.049
converge," end quote.

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:09.149
Recognizing the moral ambiguity

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.459
of functionaries' circumstances,

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:13.319
Levi cautioned against judgment of

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.359
such people writing, quote,

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.309
"This gray zone possesses an

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:19.319
incredibly complicated internal

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:21.479
structure and contains within

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:23.639
itself enough to confuse

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:25.739
our need to judge." End quote.

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.769
Esther's testimony offers an

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:30.899
entry point to discuss several

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:33.269
examples of gray zone figures

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.819
who, until relatively recently,

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:37.709
have presented scholars with

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:38.759
a taboo topic.

00:19:41.830 --> 00:19:43.839
The Jewish police in the ghetto or

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:45.819
the Ordnungsdienst, literally the

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:47.769
order service, is one

00:19:47.770 --> 00:19:49.419
group of functionaries whom Esther

00:19:49.420 --> 00:19:50.420
discusses.

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:53.409
Interestingly, as you will see,

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:55.659
Jaschael Pery, the interviewer,

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:57.189
presses Esther to pronounce a

00:19:57.190 --> 00:19:59.529
judgment, which is actually

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:01.299
a breach of Fortunoff Video

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.669
Archive's interviewer protocol.

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.499
Esther, on the other hand,

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.539
chooses to suspend judgment,

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:08.980
just as Levi suggests.

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:13.359
There are some people blaming

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:15.189
the behavior of the Jewish police

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:16.209
in the ghetto.

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:17.739
Do you have some opinion about it?

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:21.369
I have my opinion, but I

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:23.429
don't know how to explain

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:25.389
it, because some people-- I

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:27.339
say that I

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:29.349
prefer to die and not do

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:31.359
these things, but others say that

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:33.429
this is self-preservation.

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:35.289
So this is a question of-- I

00:20:35.290 --> 00:20:36.969
have to think of it much more

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:39.279
because I wasn't in this position

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:41.199
where I was to do,

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:43.599
I would call it, the dirty things.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:45.489
I did what I could.

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:47.679
I couldn't do more than I did, and

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:49.540
I couldn't save everybody's life.

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:52.839
But the other things-- eh,

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:54.819
let's say they-- they-- they-- they

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:56.469
collected the people to the

00:20:56.470 --> 00:20:58.899
gathering places to be deported.

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:01.019
Now this was an order from

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:01.969
Rumkowski.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:03.429
And then Rumkowski got the order

00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:04.539
from the German.

00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:06.299
So if you were a policeman, you

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:07.529
could have said "I don't want to do

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:09.759
it." So you would have gone

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:11.229
and you had a family.

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:12.969
So now I-- I-- I don't want to go

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:14.019
into this.

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:16.089
It's too complicated and too

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:17.469
hard to accuse.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:18.470
I don't know.

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:23.649
Evidently, she recognizes

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:25.839
that moral calculus cannot

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:27.699
account for what amounts to a

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:29.529
choiceless choice, which

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:31.209
calls for the consideration of the

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:33.489
officers' families' welfare

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:35.769
and the harm that might come to them

00:21:35.770 --> 00:21:38.019
if the officers were to disobey

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:39.020
orders.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:43.179
She takes a similarly hesitant

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:44.889
approach to judgment in her

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:46.359
discussion of one of the most

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:48.309
controversial figures in the history

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:50.469
of the Holocaust, Mordechai Chaim

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:52.299
Rumkowski, elder of the

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:53.300
Jews or Judenaltester

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:56.230
of the Litzmannstadt Ghetto.

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:01.139
I don't know how right

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.419
he was, how wrong he was because

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.099
he obeyed the German.

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:06.809
And maybe if he would not obey,

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:09.239
would nobody remained in the ghetto.

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:11.669
But if you want to be honorable

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:13.469
and stand, like to say, die on your

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.109
feet and not to live on your knees,

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:17.949
you shouldn't have had this.

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:19.829
But this is good for individuals,

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:21.839
but not to-- so the

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:23.879
history will judge him, how right

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:25.229
or how wrong he was.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:27.239
But being in the ghetto, he abused

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:28.649
his power too.

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:30.509
He was an older man and

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:32.249
he once was something in the

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:34.199
hospital. I didn't work in this

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:35.909
because I lived on one side of the

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:37.769
bridge and the doctors who lived

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:39.389
on the other side work at the other

00:22:39.390 --> 00:22:40.199
hospital.

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:42.239
It was something with the nurses.

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.249
He was going and giving

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:45.929
hits one nurse, another nurse.

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:47.789
And so-- so the doctor who

00:22:47.790 --> 00:22:48.790
was on chief

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:50.939
objected.

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:52.650
So he almost hit him too.

00:22:53.750 --> 00:22:56.459
So these were inner things,

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:57.719
painful to mention too.

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:59.369
But history will judge this.

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:01.349
I am not going to say what was right

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:02.579
and what was wrong.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:04.499
But was a lot of

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:06.659
abuses and a lot of

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:08.519
things that shouldn't have happened.

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:10.589
But as you say, you--

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:13.139
by-- it is self preservation.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:15.119
I am not going into psychology

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:16.769
right now. I don't think so we

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:18.869
have-- and I don't want even to

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:20.549
be the judge of all of this.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:24.939
She believes judgment should be

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:27.129
deferred until history renders

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:29.859
a verdict, yet by 1991,

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:31.929
when she recorded these words nearly

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:33.759
50 years after the ghetto's

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:35.829
liquidation, when would such

00:23:35.830 --> 00:23:37.749
a verdict be pronounced and who

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:39.939
would be in a position to do so?

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:41.379
Why the need for judgment in the

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:42.380
first place?

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:50.219
Again, Pery asks Esther

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:51.929
to pass judgment on a group of

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.329
functionaries, but this time

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:57.059
when it comes to the ghetto doctors,

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:59.129
there's no hesitation.

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:01.259
How would you, looking backward

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:02.260
at what

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:05.579
you experienced there as

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:07.649
a doctor with your colleagues and

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:09.809
the circumstances you had there

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.639
in the ghetto, how would you

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:12.856
judge the action there

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:16.309
of the doctors in the ghetto?

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:17.159
You mean the doctors' behavior?

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:18.059
The doctors, how did they behave?

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:19.589
And how did they act there in these

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:20.429
circumstances?

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:22.379
I would say, very

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:23.380
decent.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:26.159
Everybody tried-- you see, it's hard

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:28.139
to say to try and to do, because

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:29.082
there are two difficult things.

00:24:29.083 --> 00:24:31.469
You were-- we had paralyzed hands,

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:32.429
what we did.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:34.769
But nobody abused anything

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:36.119
but as far as I know.

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:38.039
And I am sure there wasn't

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:40.019
to abuse in any way

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:42.209
patients or neglect patients,

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:44.489
or to collaborate with German--

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:46.559
not at all, not

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:47.429
at all.

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:48.430
None of them!

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:52.699
Perhaps she was not aware, or

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:54.589
no longer recalls, but there

00:24:54.590 --> 00:24:56.509
were ghetto doctors who, under

00:24:56.510 --> 00:24:58.849
coercion, assisted Nazis

00:24:58.850 --> 00:25:00.499
in mass murder.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:02.389
Nazi officials required Jewish

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:03.979
doctors to perform medical

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:05.869
examinations on people

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:07.699
on deportation lists to

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:08.929
determine whether they were

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:10.849
appropriate candidates for the fate

00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:13.069
that awaited them at a particular

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:15.739
transport's assigned destination,

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:18.049
either a forced labor camp

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:19.729
or Chelmno.

00:25:19.730 --> 00:25:21.709
As a result, ghetto

00:25:21.710 --> 00:25:23.080
doctors like Arnold Mostowicz

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:27.169
could only protect a few individuals

00:25:27.170 --> 00:25:28.759
by finding them undesirable

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:31.099
candidates for the transport.

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:33.229
In other words, he and

00:25:33.230 --> 00:25:35.119
the other doctors who were coerced

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:37.219
into carrying out this process

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:39.049
had to decide who would

00:25:39.050 --> 00:25:41.359
be exempt from the transports.

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:43.339
And in doing so, they had

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:45.589
to determine who would live

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:46.590
and who would die.

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:52.619
While there was significant coercive

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:53.620
pressure on Mostowicz,

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:56.939
it was largely indirect,

00:25:56.940 --> 00:25:58.769
originating with ghet-- uh,

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:00.899
sorry-- with German officials

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:02.639
and conveyed to him via the

00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:04.649
so-called Jewish self-government

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.389
in the ghetto.

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:08.729
Circumstances were different

00:26:08.730 --> 00:26:10.619
in camps, however, where

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:12.569
the SS performed direct

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:14.879
supervision and thus exerted

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:17.729
the coercive force directly.

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:19.679
Although Esther's testimony does

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:21.509
not afford much insight

00:26:21.510 --> 00:26:23.459
into that dynamic, she

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:25.409
does discuss an act of

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:26.410
resistance.

00:26:27.970 --> 00:26:30.099
Once in a while, came a German

00:26:31.750 --> 00:26:33.879
official to check how

00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:35.899
was there, and I kept a little bit

00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:37.779
a double bookkeeping for

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:40.359
him. I showed the temperature

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:42.099
and everything a little bit on the

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:43.089
normal side.

00:26:43.090 --> 00:26:44.749
And the real, I didn't show them,

00:26:44.750 --> 00:26:46.949
because the majority had TB,

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:48.899
and I didn't want he should know it.

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:49.859
He didn't examine.

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:51.819
He only took it, so

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:52.839
I tried to save.

00:26:55.190 --> 00:26:56.929
Through my work on Jewish prisoner

00:26:56.930 --> 00:26:58.669
physicians, I have devised a

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:00.739
framework to add-- sorry,

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:02.869
to aid conceptualization

00:27:02.870 --> 00:27:04.519
and discussion of these

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:06.409
functionaries and potentially other

00:27:06.410 --> 00:27:08.569
functionaries behavior, the coercion

00:27:08.570 --> 00:27:09.679
resistance spectrum.

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:12.859
Unlike Levi's gray

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:15.019
zone, it sets

00:27:15.020 --> 00:27:17.029
acts of resistance firmly

00:27:17.030 --> 00:27:19.009
in the discussion, and

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:20.839
it emphasizes the dynamic of

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:22.999
coercion as opposed to the loaded

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:24.799
and largely inappropriate term

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:26.569
collaboration.

00:27:26.570 --> 00:27:28.369
The word 'spectrum' is not

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:30.499
indicative of an array

00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:32.539
between polar opposites, according

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:34.399
to which a particular act could be

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:36.499
situated and categorized.

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:38.599
Instead, it serves to draw

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:40.339
attention to the wide range of

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:42.469
possible courses of action.

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:44.419
The thrust of the model is

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:46.459
what I call historicizing

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:48.559
the gray zone and drawing attention

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:50.599
to the shifts in behavior and

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:52.909
the changes in environmental factors

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:54.919
that influence a prisoner physicians

00:27:54.920 --> 00:27:56.959
room for maneuver.

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:58.849
So in Esther's case, the

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:00.889
combination of the infrequency

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:02.839
of the SS doctor's visits

00:28:02.840 --> 00:28:04.459
and his lack of interest in

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:06.859
personally examining her patients

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:09.109
enabled her to keep two sets

00:28:09.110 --> 00:28:11.389
of medical records and thereby

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:13.309
potentially save the lives

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:15.739
of her patients with tuberculosis,

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:17.629
a diagnosis that could have been a

00:28:17.630 --> 00:28:18.630
death sentence.

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:21.379
Given that Esther's testimony

00:28:21.380 --> 00:28:23.209
offers limited insight into her

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:25.069
work as a prisoner physician at

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:27.019
Guben, and because typhus

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:28.939
largely kept her from medical work

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:30.379
at Bergen Belsen.

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:32.089
I am not able to approach my

00:28:32.090 --> 00:28:33.919
introductory essay as I

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:35.929
had hoped when I first embarked on

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:38.419
this Critical Edition project.

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:40.639
I simply do not have enough content

00:28:40.640 --> 00:28:42.509
to mobilize the coersion resistant

00:28:42.510 --> 00:28:44.719
spectrum framework in my discussion

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:46.189
of her testimony.

00:28:46.190 --> 00:28:48.469
However, as I have demonstrated

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:50.509
here, I am able to bring

00:28:50.510 --> 00:28:52.699
my dissertation research to bear

00:28:52.700 --> 00:28:54.439
in another way.

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:56.419
The insights I have gained through

00:28:56.420 --> 00:28:58.639
my macro level work on prisoner

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:00.499
physicians enable me

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:02.359
to read between the lines of

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:04.309
Esther's testimony, and

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.079
address how her presence as a

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:07.969
doctor, as well as the

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:10.759
events and places she describes,

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:13.339
sheds light on the interaction

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:15.679
between two central

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:17.599
German initiatives during

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:19.759
the Holocaust: exploitation

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:21.379
and extermination.

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:23.569
In other words, I am able to address

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:25.519
the testimony's relationship to

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:27.499
the macro-scale narrative of

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:29.069
the Holocaust.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:31.129
As such, I can point out

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:33.739
how the history behind the testimony

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:35.269
challenges the outdated and--

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:39.619
sorry, internationalist narrative

00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:42.679
that based on the following,

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:44.659
Pery evidently espouses.

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:46.579
He asks, quote,

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:48.619
"Looking backward again, about

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:50.539
the starvation, and the illness,

00:29:50.540 --> 00:29:52.459
and sickness, and the conditions

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:54.349
in the ghetto, do you

00:29:54.350 --> 00:29:56.179
think that all this was planned

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:58.729
ahead by the Germans, the situation

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:00.199
in the ghetto before the

00:30:00.200 --> 00:30:02.089
liquidation?" End quote.

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:04.609
Shortly thereafter, he adds, quote,

00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:06.709
"I read somewhere that they did,

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:08.809
they planned it purposely this way."

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:09.810
End quote.

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:13.519
Consequently, as a historiography

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:15.289
of the functionalist, intentionalist

00:30:15.290 --> 00:30:17.329
debate has given way to more

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:19.219
nuanced studies,

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:21.319
my critical edition essay will,

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:23.569
among other things, introduce

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:25.819
Jewish doctors as a lens

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:28.039
through which to view macro level

00:30:28.040 --> 00:30:29.040
developments.

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:32.219
Thanks for your attention.

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:33.419
I look forward to comments and

00:30:33.420 --> 00:30:34.420
questions.

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:43.170
Questions for Sari?

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:49.459
Maybe I missed something, but you

00:30:49.460 --> 00:30:50.490
talk about Dr. Mostowicz.

00:30:53.715 --> 00:30:53.889
Could you--

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:54.809
Elaborate?

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:56.565
Yeah, because it's a-- I don't know

00:30:56.566 --> 00:30:57.339
the context.

00:30:57.340 --> 00:30:58.630
Yeah. So he,

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:02.169
as a doctor in

00:31:02.170 --> 00:31:03.369
the Lodz ghetto,

00:31:04.480 --> 00:31:05.379
wrote a memoir.

00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:06.115
So he sheds the--

00:31:06.116 --> 00:31:06.909
Oh, okay.

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:08.949
--the most light on

00:31:08.950 --> 00:31:10.989
what went on there from the

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:12.429
medical team.

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:14.439
So I can let

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:15.499
you know about that later if you

00:31:15.500 --> 00:31:15.999
want.

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:16.989
No, I do know about it. I just--

00:31:16.990 --> 00:31:17.949
Oh, okay.

00:31:17.950 --> 00:31:18.950
Yeah.

00:31:21.070 --> 00:31:22.070
[INAUDIBLE] The name was [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:30.039
Any questions?

00:31:30.040 --> 00:31:31.040
Joshua?

00:31:31.750 --> 00:31:33.789
What for you were the most

00:31:33.790 --> 00:31:35.649
surprising revelations from

00:31:35.650 --> 00:31:37.029
your studies? What were the things

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:38.659
that you thought you might find that

00:31:38.660 --> 00:31:39.660
turned out differently?

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:44.013
Generally, when I, when I read the,

00:31:45.350 --> 00:31:47.569
the summary of her

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:49.699
Holocaust trajectory and

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:51.889
what she did, it seemed

00:31:51.890 --> 00:31:53.929
like I had hit on something

00:31:53.930 --> 00:31:56.359
golden, this is exactly

00:31:56.360 --> 00:31:58.249
what I do and what I'm

00:31:58.250 --> 00:32:00.319
going to do, in that she was

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:02.269
a prisoner physician and

00:32:02.270 --> 00:32:03.709
she also worked as a doctor in a

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:04.969
ghetto.

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:07.099
And then she worked as a doctor

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:08.959
in DP camps, something

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:10.669
that I'm going to hopefully do with

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:11.989
my next project.

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:12.990
So.

00:32:15.190 --> 00:32:17.259
That, that was just right

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:18.369
there for me.

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:20.026
And then I realized,

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:23.549
there is a little meat there, but

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:25.349
not very much.

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:27.269
So how, how can I bring

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:29.159
something else to the table

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:31.229
here so I, I can't

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:33.239
really engage in, as I said,

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:35.339
the coercion resistance spectrum

00:32:35.340 --> 00:32:37.349
framework because

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:38.759
there's not enough there.

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.319
It would, it would be too much of a

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:41.339
reach.

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:42.340
However,

00:32:43.180 --> 00:32:45.189
it occurred to me, wait,

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:47.199
I have dealt

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:49.089
with some of these issues

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:50.949
in terms of the macro scale, she

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:53.079
does provide insights

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:55.659
that I can point out.

00:32:55.660 --> 00:32:57.609
Odds are people aren't necessarily

00:32:57.610 --> 00:32:59.769
going to pick up on, oh, she,

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:01.689
she mentions that the

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:03.549
doctors were supposed to

00:33:03.550 --> 00:33:05.419
wear a certain cap

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:07.089
so they wouldn't get shot at.

00:33:07.090 --> 00:33:08.090
And,

00:33:09.310 --> 00:33:11.169
and Rumkowski brought back

00:33:11.170 --> 00:33:13.029
some doctors, or

00:33:13.030 --> 00:33:15.069
brought in some doctors from Warsaw.

00:33:16.870 --> 00:33:18.789
To me, that signals, wait

00:33:18.790 --> 00:33:19.790
a sec.

00:33:21.310 --> 00:33:24.369
They want to protect doctors.

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:25.779
The officials want to protect

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:27.602
doctors, and they facilitated

00:33:28.940 --> 00:33:30.829
the transfer of doctors from

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:32.537
the Warsaw ghetto to the Lodz

00:33:32.538 --> 00:33:33.538
ghetto. Why?

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.769
So that, these

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:39.719
are small clues that,

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:41.879
that draw my attention towards

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:42.759
a larger fact.

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:43.760
Of course,

00:33:44.620 --> 00:33:46.599
by pointing this out, I'm in

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:49.359
no way negating the horrific

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:51.219
circumstances that

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:53.299
the Jews lived in the Lodz

00:33:53.300 --> 00:33:54.300
ghetto.

00:33:55.360 --> 00:33:57.519
But the fact that this,

00:33:57.520 --> 00:33:59.499
that, that these tiny

00:33:59.500 --> 00:34:01.999
details come out

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:04.969
of the testimony, it says something.

00:34:04.970 --> 00:34:06.829
And generally, when we're

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:08.239
talking about the,

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:11.479
the horrible conditions,

00:34:11.480 --> 00:34:14.149
it wasn't entirely

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:16.039
brought on by

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:18.589
Biebow, the, the head of

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:19.590
the,

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:23.269
the ghetto administration.

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:25.369
There were plenty of people

00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:27.439
on other levels

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:29.509
working at cross purposes and

00:34:29.510 --> 00:34:30.980
for their own enrichment.

00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:34.519
That took food, that took

00:34:34.520 --> 00:34:36.649
medication that was meant

00:34:36.650 --> 00:34:38.119
for the Jews.

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:40.439
Again, horrible

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:41.519
circumstances.

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:44.859
Odds are the Nazis

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:46.959
were fine with

00:34:46.960 --> 00:34:49.178
mass death, but

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:50.439
there was something there

00:34:51.860 --> 00:34:54.499
that they wanted to do,

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:55.594
and I see that as

00:34:56.989 --> 00:34:59.059
preserving doctors lives

00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:01.219
and in some cases letting

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:03.709
medication enter the ghetto

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:05.839
and providing them with minimal

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:08.019
supplies to keep

00:35:08.020 --> 00:35:09.069
the workers alive.

00:35:09.070 --> 00:35:11.019
That's how Rumkowski kept

00:35:11.020 --> 00:35:12.789
the ghetto going.

00:35:12.790 --> 00:35:15.579
It was important to the war effort.

00:35:15.580 --> 00:35:16.580
So

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:19.569
that, that gives me

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:21.339
some, something to put in the

00:35:21.340 --> 00:35:23.769
micro-- I mean, in the macro,

00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:25.069
OK.

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:27.379
Did you look at any other doctor

00:35:27.380 --> 00:35:29.600
or prisoner physicians

00:35:31.570 --> 00:35:32.539
in the collection?

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:34.385
And if so,

00:35:36.120 --> 00:35:38.849
what is, why, again, Esther,

00:35:38.850 --> 00:35:40.769
if others had more

00:35:40.770 --> 00:35:42.179
meat?

00:35:42.180 --> 00:35:44.009
Was-- first, the first part

00:35:44.010 --> 00:35:44.849
of the question though, did you look

00:35:44.850 --> 00:35:46.199
at other physicians and did you find

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:47.309
anything else that could be useful

00:35:47.310 --> 00:35:48.310
for your research?

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:52.619
So there are testimonies

00:35:52.620 --> 00:35:54.329
of prisoner physicians, however,

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:56.159
they weren't in the regions that

00:35:56.160 --> 00:35:58.049
I specialized in, so I

00:35:58.050 --> 00:35:59.939
wasn't able to bring to bear the

00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:01.559
knowledge of the Warthegau, for

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:03.779
example, which is the location of

00:36:03.780 --> 00:36:05.189
the Lodz ghetto.

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:07.319
I had a case study

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:09.569
on the forced-- doctors

00:36:09.570 --> 00:36:11.219
in the forced labor camps for Jews

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:12.329
in the Wartegau.

00:36:12.330 --> 00:36:14.249
So I have some

00:36:14.250 --> 00:36:16.319
grasp of the mechanisms

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:17.219
going on there.

00:36:17.220 --> 00:36:19.109
What are the the dynamics?

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:20.110
And,

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:23.279
and I also have a

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:25.979
case study on the--

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:27.809
on doctors in

00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:30.209
the industrial subcamp of

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.459
Birkenau, which is parallel

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:33.460
to Guben.

00:36:34.550 --> 00:36:36.719
So, Guben's relationship to

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:37.799
Gross-Rosen.

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:39.629
So, these

00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:41.969
fit better, although,

00:36:41.970 --> 00:36:43.469
although the meat wasn't there, I

00:36:43.470 --> 00:36:45.959
felt like I could bring more

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:47.879
of what I specialized

00:36:47.880 --> 00:36:49.410
in to some extent

00:36:50.640 --> 00:36:53.159
to, to bear on,

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:54.689
on the topic.

00:36:54.690 --> 00:36:57.179
Whereas other doctors

00:36:57.180 --> 00:37:00.209
were in the General Government,

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:02.549
an area that I would love to study

00:37:02.550 --> 00:37:04.619
more, but I haven't gotten

00:37:04.620 --> 00:37:06.749
there yet, so

00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:09.089
that, that's why I chose Esther.

00:37:09.090 --> 00:37:10.090
And,

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:13.349
and still, even

00:37:13.350 --> 00:37:14.939
though I can't bring what I thought

00:37:14.940 --> 00:37:16.859
I could to it, I

00:37:16.860 --> 00:37:18.899
still think, and of course you

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:21.569
can weigh in, whether

00:37:21.570 --> 00:37:24.029
what I'm bringing is, is substantial

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:26.099
enough in terms of the idea

00:37:26.100 --> 00:37:28.799
of macro level

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:31.499
insights and

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:34.019
in micro level activities

00:37:34.020 --> 00:37:35.020
and judgments.

00:37:36.890 --> 00:37:39.049
Of course, the big thing is that

00:37:39.050 --> 00:37:40.939
to me, biggest irony here,

00:37:40.940 --> 00:37:42.979
is that by

00:37:42.980 --> 00:37:44.749
preserving life, she's helping the

00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:46.669
German war effort, which

00:37:46.670 --> 00:37:48.469
in the end would want the

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:50.239
destruction of everybody.

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:52.189
And I don't know if that's discussed

00:37:52.190 --> 00:37:54.289
a lot, at least in my

00:37:54.290 --> 00:37:56.449
reading of the Holocaust.

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:01.199
So, and of course,

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:03.029
the biggest problem was starvation.

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:04.259
I mean, people were dying of

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:05.159
starvation.

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:06.179
She could not,

00:38:07.440 --> 00:38:09.869
impact, I guess, unless

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:11.699
that was something that she

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:12.959
brought up to the people in the

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:15.029
camp, that if you don't feed people,

00:38:15.030 --> 00:38:16.679
you know, of course, then they have

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:18.209
this endless line of people, that

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:19.619
endless war.

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:21.659
So, I

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:22.979
mean, I don't know how much this is

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:23.980
discussed. This, I mean

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:26.219
it's discussed in terms of

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:28.409
Rumkowski, in terms of his

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:31.829
efforts, which preserves himself.

00:38:31.830 --> 00:38:34.019
But this is also going on

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:35.849
at this other level.

00:38:35.850 --> 00:38:37.829
You're absolutely right.

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:40.439
In fact, Esther does mention

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:43.049
that her efforts

00:38:43.050 --> 00:38:44.050
and

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:47.349
as-- as a doctor for workers

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:48.729
and what the workers were doing

00:38:48.730 --> 00:38:51.129
themselves, could be viewed

00:38:51.130 --> 00:38:52.209
as collaboration.

00:38:52.210 --> 00:38:54.369
She actually mobilizes that

00:38:54.370 --> 00:38:56.199
word, which was so

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:58.149
fascinating to me because

00:38:58.150 --> 00:39:00.099
there was an awareness that

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:02.379
the effort they put in

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:03.909
on the doctor's part to save the

00:39:03.910 --> 00:39:05.739
lives of the workers

00:39:05.740 --> 00:39:07.659
who were helping the German

00:39:07.660 --> 00:39:09.549
war effort, which, as you pointed

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:11.559
out, would keep the war

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:13.479
going, would pose greater

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:16.239
problems to a larger

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:18.139
Jewish population.

00:39:18.140 --> 00:39:20.049
That was an ethical issue.

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:21.050
So

00:39:21.880 --> 00:39:23.799
in the

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:25.629
growing scholarship

00:39:25.630 --> 00:39:27.489
on, on resistance,

00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:30.009
there's discussion

00:39:30.010 --> 00:39:31.010
of

00:39:31.880 --> 00:39:34.219
people sabotaging in, in

00:39:34.220 --> 00:39:36.229
these workplaces, so there was

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:38.389
awareness and, and, and

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:41.209
some of the workers tried to

00:39:41.210 --> 00:39:43.819
go in and sabotage

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:45.649
to hurt the--

00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:47.149
the war effort.

00:39:47.150 --> 00:39:50.329
As for the prisoner physicians

00:39:50.330 --> 00:39:52.279
there, of

00:39:52.280 --> 00:39:54.229
the ones that I studied, the memoirs

00:39:54.230 --> 00:39:55.230
I read, the

00:39:56.480 --> 00:39:58.309
testimonies they gave, there

00:39:58.310 --> 00:40:00.289
was some awareness that what they

00:40:00.290 --> 00:40:02.939
were doing was...

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:04.979
prolong-- potentially contributing

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:07.079
to prolonging the war.

00:40:07.080 --> 00:40:08.080
But at the same time,

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:11.409
they were also adhering

00:40:11.410 --> 00:40:12.610
to the Hippocratic Oath.

00:40:13.810 --> 00:40:16.269
Their job as doctors

00:40:16.270 --> 00:40:18.249
was to take

00:40:18.250 --> 00:40:19.250
care of patients.

00:40:20.250 --> 00:40:22.109
So just doing

00:40:22.110 --> 00:40:23.140
what they're supposed to do

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:26.719
had a bigger impact, but

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:28.669
the, the important thing is, is

00:40:28.670 --> 00:40:30.109
trying to help the person in front

00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:31.459
of them to the greatest extent

00:40:31.460 --> 00:40:32.460
possible.

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:36.379
Was there anything particular about

00:40:36.380 --> 00:40:37.550
Esther's testimony,

00:40:39.230 --> 00:40:40.429
the fact that she was a female

00:40:40.430 --> 00:40:41.860
doctor and a pediatrician

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:44.349
that was different from other

00:40:44.350 --> 00:40:46.299
testimonies that you looked

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:48.159
at, or does

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:50.349
it put her along

00:40:50.350 --> 00:40:52.719
with other female doctors?

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:54.609
I know it's more a deeper research

00:40:54.610 --> 00:40:56.529
topic but, just wondering what

00:40:56.530 --> 00:40:58.409
you thought of that, whether

00:40:58.410 --> 00:40:59.410
you...

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:02.059
It's something that crept into my

00:41:02.060 --> 00:41:03.956
mind when I was making the decision.

00:41:05.450 --> 00:41:07.819
The, the other testimonies of

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:09.439
doctors were men.

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:10.440
So

00:41:11.330 --> 00:41:12.979
generally I'm just a huge fan of

00:41:12.980 --> 00:41:13.980
women in medicine,

00:41:15.140 --> 00:41:16.969
on-- an underrepresented

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:19.789
group.

00:41:19.790 --> 00:41:21.769
And so the fact that I could draw

00:41:21.770 --> 00:41:24.109
attention to a woman in medicine,

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:26.299
and a woman in medicine

00:41:26.300 --> 00:41:28.729
at that time in the '30s

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:31.279
in Poland, she was overcoming

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:32.564
not just the,

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:36.319
the hurdle of being Jewish with the

00:41:36.320 --> 00:41:38.179
numerous clausus, but

00:41:38.180 --> 00:41:39.979
being a woman, studying the sciences

00:41:39.980 --> 00:41:41.959
and pursuing a

00:41:41.960 --> 00:41:43.249
course in medicine.

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:45.229
So she herself addresses

00:41:45.230 --> 00:41:47.059
this double problem, which I

00:41:47.060 --> 00:41:48.409
find fascinating.

00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:51.820
Larry.

00:41:53.570 --> 00:41:54.902
Female doctors in Auschwitz-Birkenau

00:41:57.200 --> 00:41:59.209
have written that they

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:01.259
were often asked to accompany

00:42:01.260 --> 00:42:03.169
the SS men during

00:42:03.170 --> 00:42:04.539
selections in the barracks.

00:42:05.750 --> 00:42:07.770
And they have written that

00:42:09.570 --> 00:42:11.699
they would choose the prisoners

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:13.679
whom they thought were on the verge

00:42:13.680 --> 00:42:15.449
of death anyway, they knew that the

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:17.399
people they were choosing to go into

00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:18.400
the gas chambers.

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:21.359
Do we know if Esther

00:42:21.360 --> 00:42:23.729
knew that people being transported

00:42:23.730 --> 00:42:25.679
out of the Lodz ghetto to be

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:27.449
sent to their death rather than to

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:29.329
labor camps, which is the story

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:30.330
that the Germans would tell them?

00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:33.939
OK, just based on the last thing,

00:42:33.940 --> 00:42:35.319
the story the Germans were telling--

00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:37.479
in some cases, they were actually

00:42:37.480 --> 00:42:38.537
sent to labor camps.

00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:42.609
As far as I can tell from this

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:44.619
testimony-- and I have to spend more

00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:46.899
time with the Shoah Foundation

00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:48.909
testimony-- as far as I can tell,

00:42:48.910 --> 00:42:50.859
she was not on one of the

00:42:50.860 --> 00:42:52.809
commissions

00:42:52.810 --> 00:42:55.059
for doctors who

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:57.399
looked-- who performed examinations

00:42:57.400 --> 00:43:00.189
on people on transport lists.

00:43:00.190 --> 00:43:02.199
So that's an important fact.

00:43:03.910 --> 00:43:05.769
As for her level of awareness

00:43:05.770 --> 00:43:07.389
of what was happening to the people

00:43:07.390 --> 00:43:08.470
who left the ghetto,

00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:11.559
I'm not sure when

00:43:11.560 --> 00:43:13.689
she came to the awareness that

00:43:13.690 --> 00:43:15.330
these people just don't come back.

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:18.640
That-- that's unclear.

00:43:19.670 --> 00:43:22.269
But she does talk about

00:43:22.270 --> 00:43:24.160
the horrific

00:43:25.670 --> 00:43:27.250
kinder aktion, the,

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:31.439
the Nazis going in and

00:43:31.440 --> 00:43:33.739
taking out all the

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:35.730
the children in

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:38.250
September '42,

00:43:39.640 --> 00:43:41.459
and the elderly, and

00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:43.259
clearing out the hospitals, and the

00:43:43.260 --> 00:43:44.489
violence.

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:46.319
So there's a sense that there's

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:48.179
a major danger,

00:43:48.180 --> 00:43:49.180
but

00:43:50.780 --> 00:43:52.100
I don't know for sure.

00:43:53.170 --> 00:43:55.089
But in my dissertation,

00:43:55.090 --> 00:43:57.129
I address

00:43:57.130 --> 00:43:59.349
those doctors and, and

00:43:59.350 --> 00:44:01.239
what's fascinating is that some

00:44:01.240 --> 00:44:03.249
of them didn't want to get involved

00:44:03.250 --> 00:44:05.589
in the process in the first place

00:44:05.590 --> 00:44:07.730
and they were presented with,

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:11.039
if I may, a choiceless choice.

00:44:12.350 --> 00:44:13.350
And

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:18.989
they, they could not

00:44:18.990 --> 00:44:19.990
do anything,

00:44:20.820 --> 00:44:23.019
which would mean the SS

00:44:23.020 --> 00:44:25.589
doctor would pick at random

00:44:25.590 --> 00:44:27.089
who was going to the gas chamber

00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:29.309
because X number of people

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:31.079
had to come out of the hospital.

00:44:32.240 --> 00:44:34.219
And that meant maybe some people

00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:36.259
who had a chance of surviving, one,

00:44:36.260 --> 00:44:37.909
you know, a person who is one day

00:44:37.910 --> 00:44:39.739
away from release

00:44:39.740 --> 00:44:42.589
because she

00:44:42.590 --> 00:44:45.229
was getting better,

00:44:45.230 --> 00:44:47.119
maybe that person would be sent

00:44:47.120 --> 00:44:49.009
to the gas chamber, whereas

00:44:49.010 --> 00:44:51.199
other people who are about to die

00:44:51.200 --> 00:44:52.879
would be left there.

00:44:52.880 --> 00:44:54.049
So that was one option.

00:44:54.050 --> 00:44:56.329
The other option was

00:44:56.330 --> 00:44:58.429
actually participating

00:44:58.430 --> 00:45:00.619
in these selections, knowing

00:45:00.620 --> 00:45:02.449
who was going to die

00:45:02.450 --> 00:45:04.670
of quote unquote, "natural causes,"

00:45:06.530 --> 00:45:08.419
very close to death

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:09.420
and

00:45:10.840 --> 00:45:12.465
they had no chance of recovery

00:45:12.466 --> 00:45:13.466
[INAUDIBLE]

00:45:14.830 --> 00:45:17.849
but that was actually acting.

00:45:17.850 --> 00:45:20.639
So I,

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:22.599
I'd like to put your term out there,

00:45:22.600 --> 00:45:24.989
you have to let me know whether

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:26.820
I'm conceiving of it correctly.

00:45:29.270 --> 00:45:31.489
OK, whew,

00:45:31.490 --> 00:45:33.269
very relieved there.

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:35.179
OK, we can talk to after

00:45:35.180 --> 00:45:36.949
if there are criticisms.

00:45:36.950 --> 00:45:37.950
OK.

00:45:38.090 --> 00:45:40.249
So, I'm interested in

00:45:40.250 --> 00:45:43.099
how you conceptualize resistance,

00:45:43.100 --> 00:45:44.100
because in these, in this

00:45:44.990 --> 00:45:45.990
concentrationary universe,

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:49.052
all these difficult terms

00:45:51.380 --> 00:45:52.380
are up for grabs.

00:45:53.290 --> 00:45:54.307
And so I'm interested in

00:45:56.440 --> 00:45:58.329
what your working definition is,

00:45:58.330 --> 00:46:00.309
if you have one, of resistance?

00:46:03.010 --> 00:46:04.869
Well, I believe the

00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:06.729
term has been deployed a

00:46:06.730 --> 00:46:07.929
bit too much.

00:46:10.320 --> 00:46:12.149
When it comes to the extreme

00:46:12.150 --> 00:46:14.459
of breathing was resistance

00:46:14.460 --> 00:46:16.469
because they were staying alive and

00:46:16.470 --> 00:46:17.909
the Nazis wanted to kill them all.

00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:21.809
So I back away from that

00:46:21.810 --> 00:46:23.219
extreme.

00:46:23.220 --> 00:46:25.079
I also back away from the

00:46:25.080 --> 00:46:27.209
extreme that the definition

00:46:27.210 --> 00:46:29.099
of resistance has to be

00:46:29.100 --> 00:46:30.509
armed resistance.

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:32.369
So I'm in

00:46:32.370 --> 00:46:33.749
from the extremes.

00:46:33.750 --> 00:46:34.750
And

00:46:35.610 --> 00:46:36.719
so that much I can say.

00:46:36.720 --> 00:46:38.219
I've carved out what's still a

00:46:38.220 --> 00:46:39.599
really large area.

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:42.150
I, I,

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:46.949
I eliminate the

00:46:46.950 --> 00:46:49.529
acts of resistance

00:46:49.530 --> 00:46:51.929
that are perceived by others, but

00:46:51.930 --> 00:46:54.119
the person engaged in whatever act

00:46:54.120 --> 00:46:55.120
did not

00:46:57.360 --> 00:46:59.339
think of his or her action

00:46:59.340 --> 00:47:00.479
as resistance.

00:47:00.480 --> 00:47:02.819
I look at it as, as something

00:47:02.820 --> 00:47:05.489
that, you know, someone

00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:07.319
took agency to

00:47:07.320 --> 00:47:09.329
resist. There had to be an awareness

00:47:09.330 --> 00:47:10.330
of it. So...

00:47:12.119 --> 00:47:12.130
Self-awareness.

00:47:12.131 --> 00:47:14.189
Yes, thank you. Self-awareness.

00:47:14.190 --> 00:47:15.479
So that's one way.

00:47:15.480 --> 00:47:17.339
And generally for

00:47:17.340 --> 00:47:20.159
me, it's, so it's a self-aware

00:47:21.400 --> 00:47:23.649
act that in some

00:47:23.650 --> 00:47:25.839
way, to some extent,

00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:28.779
would thwart

00:47:28.780 --> 00:47:30.879
Nazi aims, so that

00:47:30.880 --> 00:47:32.199
that's my working definition.

00:47:34.010 --> 00:47:35.010
Thanks.

00:47:37.090 --> 00:47:38.919
So you talk a little bit about

00:47:38.920 --> 00:47:40.599
during your lecture now, you talk a

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:42.819
little bit about the

00:47:42.820 --> 00:47:45.039
interview process itself and the

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:47.229
interaction between the interviewer

00:47:47.230 --> 00:47:49.539
and and the witness,

00:47:49.540 --> 00:47:51.429
and I kind of wanted to hear

00:47:51.430 --> 00:47:52.869
what you had to say about that in

00:47:52.870 --> 00:47:54.249
relation to the

00:47:55.330 --> 00:47:57.489
spectrum of coercion

00:47:57.490 --> 00:47:58.689
and resistance that you're talking

00:47:58.690 --> 00:48:00.609
about, because it seems as

00:48:00.610 --> 00:48:01.880
if, that

00:48:02.930 --> 00:48:04.469
had, have very

00:48:06.330 --> 00:48:08.339
large connection, not

00:48:08.340 --> 00:48:10.859
only impacted the interviewer

00:48:10.860 --> 00:48:13.049
seems to breach Fortunoff

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:14.879
protocols and ask

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:17.189
for for this kind of judgment, but

00:48:17.190 --> 00:48:19.099
also the, perhaps

00:48:19.100 --> 00:48:20.100
resistance that the,

00:48:21.040 --> 00:48:22.040
the witness

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:26.069
kind of portrayed

00:48:26.070 --> 00:48:27.270
within the interview itself.

00:48:30.340 --> 00:48:32.439
So one thing that I had

00:48:32.440 --> 00:48:34.779
as I was looking through

00:48:34.780 --> 00:48:36.669
the testimony, I believe they were

00:48:36.670 --> 00:48:37.670
Joanne's notes.

00:48:39.110 --> 00:48:41.179
And in brackets

00:48:41.180 --> 00:48:42.854
in many places along the way,

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:45.600
the comments were like

00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:49.049
he's, he's not supposed to ask

00:48:49.050 --> 00:48:49.829
that.

00:48:49.830 --> 00:48:52.109
How is, how is the witness

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:53.999
supposed to know the answer to

00:48:54.000 --> 00:48:55.979
this big historiographical

00:48:55.980 --> 00:48:56.980
question?

00:48:58.170 --> 00:49:00.299
And I could feel

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:01.079
the frustration.

00:49:01.080 --> 00:49:03.459
I'm assuming it's, it's you.

00:49:03.460 --> 00:49:04.529
Yeah.

00:49:04.530 --> 00:49:06.829
Just like, what is this guy doing?

00:49:08.070 --> 00:49:10.229
He is actively inserting

00:49:10.230 --> 00:49:12.209
himself. He is asking these big

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:14.099
questions that someone

00:49:14.100 --> 00:49:15.929
in, in her own life

00:49:15.930 --> 00:49:17.219
and her-- that's what she's the

00:49:17.220 --> 00:49:18.689
expert on. That's what she's there

00:49:18.690 --> 00:49:19.690
to talk about.

00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:22.409
But he was like, oh, yeah,

00:49:22.410 --> 00:49:24.479
I read that,

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:26.399
that, you know, the Germans have

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:27.749
this in mind from the beginning.

00:49:27.750 --> 00:49:29.069
Well, good for you.

00:49:29.070 --> 00:49:30.070
But

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:32.789
this is about Esther.

00:49:32.790 --> 00:49:34.829
So, so

00:49:34.830 --> 00:49:36.449
it was, it was frustrating for me

00:49:36.450 --> 00:49:38.429
and I felt for Joanne through those

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:40.469
notes that she probably

00:49:40.470 --> 00:49:42.119
wanted to reach her hand through the

00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:43.259
screen.

00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:44.909
And I don't know what.

00:49:48.570 --> 00:49:50.399
He, he,

00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:52.589
as far as I can recall, he didn't

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:54.719
engage once she put that double

00:49:54.720 --> 00:49:56.519
bookkeeping out there.

00:49:56.520 --> 00:49:58.439
That was a point. He, he, he didn't

00:49:58.440 --> 00:49:59.440
latch onto that.

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:03.359
I, I would need to watch

00:50:03.360 --> 00:50:05.849
the whole thing in its entirety

00:50:05.850 --> 00:50:07.679
again. But

00:50:07.680 --> 00:50:09.659
I'm not sure whether he

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:11.579
asked, oh, was there

00:50:11.580 --> 00:50:12.580
resistance.

00:50:13.680 --> 00:50:15.629
He, based on what I've

00:50:15.630 --> 00:50:17.609
pulled, he seemed to be

00:50:17.610 --> 00:50:19.829
more fixated on

00:50:19.830 --> 00:50:22.679
the, the idea of the collaboration.

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:26.849
But that's a really interesting

00:50:26.850 --> 00:50:28.679
point. And another thing

00:50:28.680 --> 00:50:31.169
to say in terms of, of

00:50:31.170 --> 00:50:33.419
my construction of the

00:50:33.420 --> 00:50:34.420
spectrum model,

00:50:35.790 --> 00:50:37.529
based on what he's showing, I think

00:50:37.530 --> 00:50:39.359
he's nowhere near that conceptual

00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:41.369
point of nuance.

00:50:41.370 --> 00:50:43.199
He just simply is,

00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:44.579
is not there.

00:50:44.580 --> 00:50:45.580
So.

00:50:47.260 --> 00:50:49.149
So I can't

00:50:49.150 --> 00:50:51.339
really bring it into his universe

00:50:51.340 --> 00:50:53.190
because he's, he's just not on it.

00:50:55.650 --> 00:50:56.650
I'm going to have to...one

00:50:59.050 --> 00:50:59.529
more question.

00:50:59.530 --> 00:51:00.530
OK.

00:51:01.210 --> 00:51:03.139
Were the prisoner doctors,

00:51:03.140 --> 00:51:05.169
I sympathize for the decisions

00:51:05.170 --> 00:51:07.299
that they had to make at

00:51:07.300 --> 00:51:08.569
times.

00:51:08.570 --> 00:51:10.489
And I was wondering if they were

00:51:10.490 --> 00:51:12.379
sufficiently valuable enough

00:51:12.380 --> 00:51:13.549
to the Germans that they were

00:51:13.550 --> 00:51:15.619
treated in a way to keep them alive.

00:51:17.030 --> 00:51:18.260
That's a great question.

00:51:19.640 --> 00:51:21.319
It depends on the camp.

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:22.880
It depends where, when.

00:51:25.010 --> 00:51:27.049
Generally they had extra

00:51:27.050 --> 00:51:28.069
food.

00:51:28.070 --> 00:51:31.219
In some cases that was prescribed,

00:51:31.220 --> 00:51:33.139
extra rations, to keep

00:51:33.140 --> 00:51:35.419
them alive, to do their doctorly

00:51:35.420 --> 00:51:36.420
thing.

00:51:37.010 --> 00:51:39.650
In other cases, it was,

00:51:41.570 --> 00:51:42.860
they ate the food

00:51:44.300 --> 00:51:46.279
for prisoners

00:51:46.280 --> 00:51:47.280
who couldn't eat it.

00:51:49.070 --> 00:51:50.899
And they also shared it with, with

00:51:50.900 --> 00:51:53.150
people in the barracks, so,

00:51:54.410 --> 00:51:56.479
so generally they

00:51:56.480 --> 00:51:57.829
were pretty well fed.

00:51:57.830 --> 00:51:59.809
The fact, just the fact that they

00:51:59.810 --> 00:52:01.849
worked inside as opposed

00:52:01.850 --> 00:52:04.639
to on outside labor commando,

00:52:04.640 --> 00:52:06.147
that kept them alive longer

00:52:07.230 --> 00:52:08.910
to, to do what they were doing.

00:52:11.690 --> 00:52:13.969
And because

00:52:13.970 --> 00:52:15.829
they didn't work under

00:52:15.830 --> 00:52:17.899
the cruel, watchful

00:52:17.900 --> 00:52:20.299
eye of a horrible

00:52:20.300 --> 00:52:23.599
kapo, they were generally

00:52:23.600 --> 00:52:25.459
under under

00:52:25.460 --> 00:52:27.439
the watch of a head

00:52:27.440 --> 00:52:29.359
doctor, whether it's a Jewish

00:52:29.360 --> 00:52:31.309
doctor or not, the

00:52:31.310 --> 00:52:33.589
business within these,

00:52:33.590 --> 00:52:34.939
they were called hospitals or

00:52:34.940 --> 00:52:35.940
clinics or...

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:38.685
Revier.

00:52:38.686 --> 00:52:39.686
Yeah.

00:52:40.250 --> 00:52:40.336
Revier.

00:52:40.337 --> 00:52:41.329
Yes, that's the term that comes up,

00:52:41.330 --> 00:52:42.979
I'm try-- and Häftlings

00:52:42.980 --> 00:52:45.469
Krankenbauer, Krankenbauer, KB,

00:52:45.470 --> 00:52:47.389
so yeah.

00:52:47.390 --> 00:52:49.699
So I'm just trying to translate the,

00:52:49.700 --> 00:52:50.700
the different meanings.

00:52:53.110 --> 00:52:54.110
So.

00:52:55.620 --> 00:52:57.419
They, they weren't exposed to the

00:52:57.420 --> 00:52:58.420
same kind of violence.

00:52:59.640 --> 00:53:01.739
So how conscious

00:53:01.740 --> 00:53:03.569
of a decision that

00:53:03.570 --> 00:53:05.999
was on the part of

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:07.919
camp leadership, I

00:53:07.920 --> 00:53:08.920
can't tell you,

00:53:09.870 --> 00:53:11.759
but it was a fact that they

00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:14.759
were spared some of the disastrous,

00:53:14.760 --> 00:53:16.859
horrific, horrific circumstances

00:53:16.860 --> 00:53:18.929
that the average prisoner

00:53:18.930 --> 00:53:19.930
had to confront.

00:53:21.260 --> 00:53:23.089
I just want to address this

00:53:23.090 --> 00:53:24.499
point, I think the most important

00:53:24.500 --> 00:53:25.500
thing you said was in the beginning

00:53:26.270 --> 00:53:27.270
and

00:53:28.640 --> 00:53:29.640
[INAUDIBLE] plenty of doctors

00:53:30.740 --> 00:53:32.299
were selected as soon as they

00:53:32.300 --> 00:53:34.159
arrived, for death, even though

00:53:34.160 --> 00:53:36.349
they were known to be physicians.

00:53:36.350 --> 00:53:38.419
Then it just depended where and

00:53:38.420 --> 00:53:39.209
when.

00:53:39.210 --> 00:53:41.329
Maybe [INAUDIBLE] too.

00:53:41.330 --> 00:53:43.249
No, it was, it's, just, what was

00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:45.400
happening at that moment in time.

00:53:46.530 --> 00:53:48.989
You know, it's so random,

00:53:48.990 --> 00:53:51.659
it's, it's not systematic,

00:53:51.660 --> 00:53:53.609
we always think the Nazi

00:53:53.610 --> 00:53:55.859
regime was rational,

00:53:55.860 --> 00:53:57.739
systematic, it was not.

00:53:57.740 --> 00:53:58.740
It was actually quite chaotic.

00:54:00.479 --> 00:54:02.399
Not in the Warthegau when it came to

00:54:02.400 --> 00:54:03.479
doctors.

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:05.309
Right. So so,

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:05.759
yes,

00:54:05.760 --> 00:54:08.339
I've seen testimonies

00:54:08.340 --> 00:54:10.469
of people whose parents

00:54:10.470 --> 00:54:11.399
were physicians.

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:11.999
Yes.

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:14.129
And the person selecting them knew

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:15.089
they were physicians--

00:54:15.090 --> 00:54:16.289
But said, you're too, you're too

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:17.399
old. You've lived.

00:54:17.400 --> 00:54:18.659
There's one--

00:54:18.660 --> 00:54:19.660
But they were sent to death.

00:54:20.580 --> 00:54:22.139
There's one testimony.

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:23.699
I think her name is Gertrude

00:54:23.700 --> 00:54:24.700
Mossberg.

00:54:25.710 --> 00:54:26.999
She said, Oh, but my father's a

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:28.000
physician.

00:54:28.630 --> 00:54:29.969
Oh, how old is he?

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:31.589
And she states the age.

00:54:31.590 --> 00:54:32.879
Oh, he's lived long enough.

00:54:34.140 --> 00:54:35.549
So you're, you're right.

00:54:36.630 --> 00:54:38.459
But at the same time,

00:54:38.460 --> 00:54:40.709
again, depending on when,

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:43.259
if we're talking, if we're talking

00:54:43.260 --> 00:54:44.260
Birkenau.

00:54:44.710 --> 00:54:46.809
There was a stage when

00:54:46.810 --> 00:54:49.029
the SS physicians

00:54:49.030 --> 00:54:50.053
performing selections

00:54:51.390 --> 00:54:53.279
were told to spare

00:54:53.280 --> 00:54:55.199
the lives of Jewish doctors, and

00:54:55.200 --> 00:54:57.209
this was in '44 when labor

00:54:57.210 --> 00:54:58.979
was crucial.

00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:00.839
And those people

00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:03.419
were transferred

00:55:03.420 --> 00:55:06.059
from Birkenau

00:55:06.060 --> 00:55:07.949
to Monowitz, to

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:09.899
the other subcamps, so there

00:55:09.900 --> 00:55:11.969
was a concerted effort

00:55:11.970 --> 00:55:14.009
and you could see in

00:55:14.010 --> 00:55:16.379
the documentation, this person,

00:55:16.380 --> 00:55:18.269
this doctor is transferred on the

00:55:18.270 --> 00:55:20.279
order of the

00:55:20.280 --> 00:55:22.169
head physician,

00:55:22.170 --> 00:55:24.029
the head SS physician.

00:55:24.030 --> 00:55:26.159
So, so you're

00:55:26.160 --> 00:55:27.459
right. It changed.

00:55:27.460 --> 00:55:29.549
But there were

00:55:29.550 --> 00:55:32.849
attempts at systematizing

00:55:32.850 --> 00:55:34.949
the selection, the positive

00:55:34.950 --> 00:55:36.200
selection of, of doctors.

00:55:38.910 --> 00:55:40.700
OK, well, thank you, Sari.

